{"step_index":317,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-01T17:01:11Z","thinking":"File `MXZ6_261001_261001.txt` parsed successfully, containing 157 five-minute bars. Data spans from 06:55:00 to 19:55:00 on 2026-10-01, with complete open/high/low/close/volume data. The file name's structure suggests a possible Russian origin.\n\n\n\"Lupa\" integrates LuaJIT, a Just-In-Time compiler for Lua, directly into a Python process via a C-library implemented in Cython. This contrasts with external process execution and parsing of output; Lupa provides a shared memory space for fast Lua code execution. The `LuaRuntime()` facilitates creating a Lua virtual machine that can run code quickly, allowing variables to be passed between Lua and Python.\n\n\nThe goal is to implement dynamic file loading based on user specifications.  The program should first scan a specified directory for the most recent data file, falling back to a default directory if the specified directory is empty.  A file selection menu item needs to be added for manual data loading, enabling immediate graph updates upon file selection.\n\n\nConsidered two primary candle rendering methods. The first uses batching with individual `PlotCurveItem` and `BarGraphItem` objects for wicks and bodies, respectively, significantly reducing the number of graphical objects. The second involves a custom graphics item leveraging `QPainter` for direct drawing and caching via `QPicture` for potentially high performance. Need to compare these two options to determine the optimal rendering strategy.\n\n\nInvestigating the `CandlestickItem` implementation, a `QPicture` caches drawing operations for efficient re-rendering during zoom and pan. This is done within `generatePicture()`, ensuring a single graphics object handles all candle data. The `boundingRect()` and axis limits are automatically managed by the item, simplifying integration.\n\n\nImplemented direct drawing and caching using `QPicture` for candlestick rendering.  This approach allows for efficient re-rendering during zoom and pan operations, as drawing commands are pre-computed. Decided on representing the X-axis using candle ordinal numbers, instead of raw timestamps, to avoid gaps in time series data. The X-axis formatting will then handle the conversion to readable time strings.\n\n\nX-axis uses candle ordinal numbers (0, 1, 2, ... N-1) to ensure continuous, gap-free candle representation, mirroring the appearance of TradingView and QUIK.  This sequential numbering guarantees that MACD and moving averages will align perfectly with the candles.\n\nThe focus is now on showing the file and implementing dynamic file loading (auto-search and manual selection) according to the user's \"Давай\" instruction. The Lua code remains untouched as it already parses the data file efficiently.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":".venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -c \"from PyQt6 import QtCore, QtGui; pic = QtGui.QPicture(); p = QtGui.QPainter(pic); p.drawLine(QtCore.QPointF(0, 0), QtCore.QPointF(10, 10)); p.end(); print('QPicture works, rect:', pic.boundingRect())\"","Cwd":"Y:\\SIGNAL","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Testing QPicture on PyQt6","toolSummary":"Test QPicture on PyQt6"}}]}
